{"cases":[{"target_alias":"SRC_001","attack":true,"case":"The problem asks 'Where a claim can be tested by counting or by execution, say exactly what to count or execute.' SRC_001 defines its mechanism purely in observational terms—generating a counter-example and resolving it—without specifying any executable or countable operation. It gives no concrete recipe for claims that are testable by counting or execution, so it fails to meet the problem's evidentiary standard.","counterexample":null},{"target_alias":"SRC_002","attack":true,"case":"SRC_002's mechanism asserts that a stronger single engine can catch its own sophisticated mistakes because it 'retains the specific internal trace of the error's origin,' but it provides no causal mechanism for how that trace is retrieved and applied in a single run's record. The claim is asserted, not derived; there is no process linking self-trace retention to detection that a critic could test by counting or execution.","counterexample":null},{"target_alias":"SRC_003","attack":true,"case":"SRC_003 argues that a weaker independent engine may give a false positive due to an inability to parse the error, while a stronger single engine can leverage higher-order reasoning to pinpoint its own failure mode. But it never identifies what in-record evidence would distinguish a genuine self-correction from a restatement; the mechanism presupposes the very independence distinction the problem asks it to evidence. No counting or execution test is supplied.","counterexample":null},{"target_alias":"SRC_004","attack":true,"case":"SRC_004's information-theoretic isolation mechanism claims functional independence if the checker reconstructs logic from scratch and it matches the claim. But mere reconstruction matching is not causal evidence of verification: a second engine can reproduce a false derivation by coincidence or by following the same flawed inference. The target gives no executable or countable criterion—e.g., running the reconstruction against held-out tests—to distinguish genuine verification from restatement.","counterexample":null}]}